      "they_reproduce_extremely_slowly_10_12_": "They reproduce extremely slowly — ~10-12 eggs per year per female, after a 15+ year maturation",
      "this_cell_paper_revealed_unprecedented": "This Cell paper revealed UNPRECEDENTED levels of RNA editing in cephalopod nervous systems — 1000x more than in mammals.",
      "this_indo_pacific_octopus_species_was_": "This Indo-Pacific octopus species was newly described in 1998. Norman et al. documented its remarkable flexibility — impersonating different species depending on which predator was present.",
      "this_is_part_of_why_octopuses_prefer": "This is part of why octopuses prefer ",
      "this_simulator_was_built_in_2025_2026_": "This simulator was built in 2025-2026 by Aaron Pomeranz, drawing on the published work of dozens of cephalopod biologists. Specific thanks to:\n\n• Jennifer Mather (University of Lethbridge) — foundational welfare + cognition research\n• Roger Hanlon (MBL Woods Hole) — camouflage + display behavior\n• Bret Grasse (MBL Woods Hole) — cephalopod husbandry\n• Sy Montgomery — public engagement\n• Z. Yan Wang (UWa) — optic gland research\n• Robyn Crook (SFSU) — welfare + pain research\n• Jonathan Birch (LSE) — sentience review + policy\n• Alexandra Schnell (Cambridge) — cuttlefish cognition\n• The MBARI team — deep-sea cephalopod research\n• The Birch et al. 2021 LSE report — foundational welfare evidence\n\nAll cited research is publicly available. Every claim in the simulator can be traced back to peer-reviewed publication. The tool is built on the shoulders of these researchers + many others.\n\nIf you find errors or have suggestions, please email aaron@alloflow.com.",
      "this_simulator_was_built_with_el_educa": "This simulator was built with EL Education classroom rhythms in mind (crew → workshop → reflection). Every lesson here follows that ",
      "this_tool_was_built_by_aaron_pomeranz_": "This tool was built by Aaron Pomeranz (PsyD, school psychologist) at Portland Public Schools, working at King Middle School — an EL Education school in Portland, Maine. It is designed for the EL Education model: students-as-scientists, crew + critique + reflection, evidence-based reasoning, ethical engagement.\n\nThe tool is provided FREE. No login. No data collection. No FERPA surface. Browser-based + localStorage-only.\n\nIt was built because cephalopod biology is the perfect entry into modern biology + ecology + ethics — and existing curricular resources don't adequately cover it. School science textbooks devote 1-2 pages to cephalopods. Documentaries are excellent but expensive. Aquarium visits are time-limited.\n\nThis simulator makes cephalopod biology engaging, accessible, and pedagogically deep. It's not a replacement for hands-on field experience or aquarium visits. It IS a substantial supplement that any teacher can use.\n\nIf you're using this tool: please share your experiences. Email aaron@allofow.com or use the feedback link in the resource tab. The tool will continue to develop based on classroom use.",
